---
/**
 * The frame around the request path: one spine, top to bottom, with the
 * stages a call meets slotted into it in order.
 *
 * It replaces a static SVG of the same path. The SVG had to carry every
 * caption at once, so it either shouted or went unreadable, and the
 * explanations lived in a prose section further down the page — a reader had
 * to hold the picture in their head while scrolling to the words.
 *
 * Visually it is the Learn hub's map, from the shared `.learn-map` material
 * in global.css: same palette, same panel, same control. One path, drawn in
 * two places, should not look like two products.
 *
 * The spine is a single line the stages sit on, because the claim the page is
 * making is that there is exactly one path. Anything drawn off to the side
 * would be arguing against the page.
 */
---

<div class="rp learn-map">
    <ol class="rp-flow">
        <slot />
    </ol>
</div>

<style>
    .rp {
        margin: 2rem 0;
        /* Flat, and darker than the cards on it. Stacking a gradient here
           under the seam's own tint made two fades cross in the middle of the
           panel, which read as a smudge rather than as depth. */
        background: var(--sunken);
        border: 1px solid var(--line);
        border-radius: var(--r);
        padding: 18px 18px 20px;
    }

    .rp-flow {
        list-style: none;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 10px;
        position: relative;
    }

    /* The spine. One line, behind the cards — it is the page's whole argument,
       so it is drawn once and never breaks. 26px = card padding (16px) + half
       a 22px ordinal, so the seam's ordinals sit on it. */
    .rp-flow::before {
        content: "";
        position: absolute;
        left: 26px;
        top: 18px;
        bottom: 18px;
        width: 2px;
        border-radius: 2px;
        background: linear-gradient(
            180deg,
            transparent,
            var(--line-2) 6%,
            var(--line-2) 94%,
            transparent
        );
    }
</style>
